Setup
- MySQL root/root was committed in app.py's history. The MySQL code is gone now, but change that password if that database is ever reachable from another machine.

Price accuracy
- prices.csv values are eBay sold prices. Local flip prices may be lower or higher. Refresh the data every 1-2 weeks.
- coolers, PSUs, storage and cases have no price data, so they count as $0 until you type a value on the evaluate page.
  Desktop and combo profit is understated by their value. Collecting sold prices for them would fix it.
- prebuilt desktops (Dell OptiPlex, HP, Lenovo, mini PCs) never have an identifiable board, so the board counts as $0
  and the listing says "Prebuilt PC" instead of "not identified"
- no price data for older Radeon cards (R9, R7, HD series), mobile CPUs (i5-8265U and other U-series) or
  Core 2 / Pentium chips. They're named in the breakdown but count as $0.
- RAM with no kit layout ("16GB DDR4") is priced as the average 16GB DDR4 kit. A known layout ("2x8GB") gets its own price.

sold_parser.py extractors (found while testing; scanner works around these)
- they have to keep making the same names as prices.csv, so fix them only together with a prices.csv rebuild
- extract_mobo needs a brand in the title. scanner.mobo_by_chipset prices "B660M Motherboard" as an average B660M board.
- extract_mobo keeps words like "combo" and "bundle" in the model name. scanner.clean_title strips them.
- extract_mobo drops words before a dashed chipset: "ASUS TUF GAMING Z490-PLUS" becomes "ASUS Z490-PLUS",
  which misses the exact price and uses the chipset fallback
- extract_ram reads GPU memory as RAM ("RTX 3070 8GB 32GB DDR4" -> 8GB) and extract_mobo picks up storage words ("1TB").
  scanner.clean_title strips these before extracting.
- extract_models reads "Xeon E5-2680 v4" as "Xeon -2680 v4". prices.csv uses the same name, so it still matches.

Ideas
- mixed CPU lots ("LOT OF 6- i5 2nd Gen- x2 2400- x3 2320- x1 2500") aren't priced. Summing each model would work.
- accessories are caught from the title ("EMPTY BOX", "box only", "fans for", "cooler for Ryzen"). A cooler or
  fan listed without those words would still be valued as the part.
- vendor-locked chips (Threadripper PRO and EPYC pulled from Lenovo or Dell workstations) look like deals but only
  work in that brand's boards. Check the description for "locked" or "PSB".
- the Desktop search now reads descriptions too. The first scan after starting app.py fetches up to 100 descriptions
  (about 15 to 20 seconds). They're saved in data/details_cache.json for 12 hours.
